@packages/core/src/model/retrieval.sigil import { SigilRetrievalModel }

component SigilEvaluatorRetrievalBrief {
  goal {
    Derive one compact, component-oriented evaluator brief from purpose retrieval
    without changing the complete result retained by compilation.
  }

  interface {
    EvaluatorRetrievalBrief {
      deriveEvaluatorRetrievalBrief(PurposeRetrievalResult) returns one immutable
      brief containing target, purpose, raw retrieval fingerprint, selected and
      related component contracts, glossary entries, allowed direct-read paths, and
      an LLM-friendly projection of the selected dependency graph.

      The brief renders as deterministic compact Markdown-like records for an
      evaluator prompt. It has no JSON object serialization, repeated schema headers,
      raw evidence records, inclusion reasons, exclusions, aggregated context, or
      implementation-source text.

      Component contracts group semantic units below their component and concept
      identity. A component is shown once even when several graph relationships
      select it. Expansions merge into that component rather than creating another
      component heading.

      The dependency graph is one optional Markdown section after component
      contracts. Each line uses readable component labels and one relation arrow;
      repeated source labels are grouped as an adjacency list. A declaration path
      disambiguates equal component names. It contains no raw node or edge JSON,
      schema keywords, or stable identities.

      Allowed direct-read paths are the normalized unique paths from the selected
      component and selected raw evidence. They are navigation permissions for
      targeted verification, not additional evaluation membership.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilEvaluatorRetrievalBrief {
  logic {
    EvaluatorRetrievalBrief {
      Derive component contracts, glossary entries, ownership links, and stable
      presentation order from SigilRetrievalModel RetrievalProjection.

      Derive the dependency graph directly from the raw selected graph instead of
      inferring it from component links. Group its selected edges under readable
      source-component labels, preserve raw edge order within each source, and omit
      only isolated non-component nodes that convey no component relationship.

      Render one short provenance line, one target line, optional compact component
      sections, one dependency-graph section, optional glossary, and one
      allowed-paths section. Omit every empty section and never repeat a field label
      per semantic unit when its component or concept heading already gives that
      context.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    EvaluatorRetrievalBrief {
      The raw PurposeRetrievalResult remains the authoritative membership,
      completeness, snapshot, and provenance record. Its fingerprint is retained
      verbatim in the brief and compiler request; the brief never replaces or
      recomputes it.

      A provider receives only the rendered brief and allowed direct-read paths, not
      the raw retrieval result. Compiler validation continues to bind the raw result
      to target, purpose, and workspace snapshot before provider invocation.

      Direct reads may verify an included contract, ownership target, or graph
      relation. They may not add ordinary finding evidence outside the raw result's
      selected membership.

      Every rendered component and graph relation resolves to the exact raw selected
      node and edge used to derive it. Invalid or incomplete raw retrieval prevents
      brief derivation and provider invocation.

      Rendering is deterministic for byte-identical raw retrieval input. It does not
      depend on the CLI renderer, workspace reloads, or provider behavior.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    SeparateEvaluatorRetrievalBrief {
      Decision: Add a compiler-owned evaluator brief distinct from CLI retrieval
      Markdown and derive it from raw purpose retrieval plus its projection.

      Scope: Governs compiler-to-provider retrieval presentation; excludes CLI output
      compatibility, raw purpose-retrieval v1, and report storage.

      Rationale: CLI Markdown is optimized for interactive retrieval, whereas an
      evaluator needs compact prompt evidence, explicit provenance, and readable
      graph topology without JSON field-name repetition.

      Trade-offs: The compiler maintains a second renderer, while evaluator input is
      substantially smaller and avoids coupling provider prompts to CLI presentation.

      Discarded alternatives: Serializing raw JSON and reusing CLI Markdown unchanged
      were rejected because the former duplicates evidence and field names, while the
      latter does not expose a compact graph projection in the needed form.

      Consequences: The CLI and compiler may evolve distinct presentations while
      sharing one authoritative PurposeRetrievalResult and retrieval fingerprint.
    }

    MarkdownDependencyGraph {
      Decision: Project the selected raw graph into one compact Markdown dependency
      section rather than sending raw node and edge records.

      Scope: Governs dependency-topology presentation; excludes traversal beyond
      selected retrieval membership and graph-derived finding semantics.

      Rationale: Component contracts show meaning, while readable relation arrows
      make dependency direction and ownership relationships inspectable without JSON
      field names, stable-identity noise, repeated node metadata, or a separate graph
      request.

      Consequences: Evaluators can reason about the selected topology directly but
      still require a concrete architectural consequence for a finding.
    }
  }

  cases {
    EvaluatorRetrievalBrief {
      An implementation-purpose result with repeated implementation-source evidence
      retains its raw fingerprint and allowed source path once, but sends no source
      text or duplicate context section to the provider.

      A component that is both a dependency and a cycle member renders one compact
      contract and one grouped graph line for every selected relation that describes
      it.

      A result with no glossary, ownership, or graph edges omits those empty brief
      sections while retaining provenance, target, and selected component contract.

      Two evaluators for the same raw retrieval fingerprint receive byte-identical
      brief text, graph projection, and allowed direct-read paths.
    }
  }
}
